Localized electrochemical activity was further examined using SVET ( Fig. 19 
                         ). The SVET images of the original and pre-strained samples show clear differences in current density distribution over time during immersion in 3.5 wt.% NaCl solution. At the initial stage (0 h), the pre-strained sample exhibits higher localized current densities compared to the original sample, indicating a more active corrosion process. This can be attributed to the microstructural changes induced by pre-straining, which promote anodic dissolution. At 1 h, the current density of the pre-strained sample decreases due to the formation of a corrosion product film on its surface, as confirmed by post-immersion EDS analysis ( Fig. 20 
                         ). EDS results reveal that localized agglomerations (Points 1) are rich in oxygen, while other areas (Point 2) show a more uniform, oxygen-lean layer, suggesting a partially protective film likely composed of iron (oxy)hydroxides such as FeOOH. However, starting from 2 h, the corrosion product film begins to break down, leading to a rapid increase in localized corrosion current density. This behavior is indicative of pitting corrosion, which is consistent with earlier observations of localized corrosion in the pre-strained sample ( Section 3.4 ). In contrast, the original sample exhibits a more uniform and stable current density over time, reflecting its more homogeneous microstructure and better resistance to localized corrosion.
